{
  "title": "Overview of IT Network Types and Considerations",
  "description": "This summary provides an overview of various IT network types and their considerations. The most basic type of network is the Local Area Network (LAN), which is a wired network offering secure and centralized data management. A wireless version of LAN, commonly known as Wi-Fi, uses radiofrequency technologies and is defined by IEEE standards, with Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax) being the latest. Wide Area Networks (WANs), such as the Internet, connect geographically dispersed locations and are managed by network administrators. A typical network setup includes switches, routers, and firewalls to ensure secure communication between LAN and WAN. Wireless networks offer minimal installation costs and flexibility in extending coverage but are prone to interference from environmental factors like steel walls, weather conditions, and radio signals. They also face challenges such as slower speeds compared to wired networks and potential congestion when too many users share the same frequency. Wired networks, while requiring additional infrastructure, provide higher speeds and reliability. The choice between wired and wireless networks depends on the specific context and application requirements."
}
