{
  "title": "Planned Product Cost (PPC) Split Documentation",
  "description": {
    "scope_and_purpose": "The Planned Product Cost (PPC) split is part of the L3(V) process 'Prepare Result Accounting' and explains the posting logic for Cost of Goods Sold (COGS) at goods issue. It is a central cross-company code customization, requiring no user actions.",
    "process_description_and_explanation": {
      "premise": "In S/4HANA, result accounting is account-based (Margin Analysis) rather than costing-based as in R/3. Planned Cost of Sales (PCOS) is booked in Result Accounting using G/L accounts. To achieve a detailed structure, the Cost of Sales must be split into different accounts.",
      "cost_structure": "The PPC split in S/4HANA is based on the auxiliary cost component structure rather than the classic PPC structure (MAT + other variable costs + fixed costs) used in R/3. Manual tariffs in cost centers and costings without quantity structure are not allowed for materials to be sold, as the system cannot derive the required structure.",
      "customizing": {
        "overview": "Customizing is located in the Finance Accounting menu.",
        "requirements": [
          "Every account used for customer deliveries (MVT 601) must have an entry in the cost splitting profile.",
          "Each account must have a target account assigned for each element of the auxiliary cost structure.",
          "If no auxiliary cost split is found for a material, all costs are mapped to item 260 (default checkbox).",
          "An offset account is also required."
        ]
      },
      "process": "When shipping is completed, movement type 601 generates two accounting documents with account assignment to the Profitability segment. No user intervention is required.",
      "remark": "In the P&L account, Cost of Goods Sold (COGS) is entered at the time of goods issue, which may cause a time mismatch between Sales Revenues and COGS. However, the time mismatch between external and internal accounting, as seen in R/3, is no longer possible."
    }
  }
}
